<?xml version='1.0' encoding='utf-8'?>

<section xml:id="sec_soccer_competition_strategy" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Soccer Competition: Multi-Robot Task Strategy</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>In the soccer competition lab, a VEX robot must locate a ball, approach it, and strike it toward a goal while sharing the field with teammates and an opposing robot. This is a reactive-navigation problem layered with a scoring objective.</p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec_soccer_state_machine">
    <title>A Search-Approach-Align-Kick State Machine</title>
    <p>A practical strategy breaks the task into four states:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><p><em class="emphasis">Search:</em> rotate or patrol until the vision system reports a ball detection.</p></li>
      <li><p><em class="emphasis">Approach:</em> drive toward the ball's estimated position, re-planning as the detection updates.</p></li>
      <li><p><em class="emphasis">Align:</em> rotate in place once close to the ball so the kicking mechanism faces the goal.</p></li>
      <li><p><em class="emphasis">Kick:</em> trigger the actuator, then return to Search.</p></li>
    </ul>
    <p>Each transition depends on a measured condition, such as ball distance falling below a threshold, rather than a fixed timer. This keeps the robot responsive if the ball moves before contact.</p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec_soccer_opponent_handling">
    <title>Sharing the Field with Other Robots</title>
    <p>Because opponents and teammates are moving obstacles, the same reactive-avoidance rules from earlier chapters apply during the Approach state: if another robot enters the danger threshold, the controller should steer around it before resuming pursuit of the ball. Treating other robots as dynamic obstacles, rather than ignoring them, prevents collisions during fast plays.</p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec_soccer_tradeoffs">
    <title>Offense, Defense, and Timing Tradeoffs</title>
    <p>A robot that always chases the ball can leave its own goal undefended. Teams often assign roles, such as one robot biased toward offense and another biased toward blocking the goal mouth, and switch roles based on which side of the field the ball is on. These role-assignment rules are a lightweight form of the task planning introduced earlier in the course.</p>
  </subsection>
</section>
